File: aruhan_security_ai_module.support
Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
Module: ARUHAN Security AI (Y‑Prime Edition)
1. Overview
The Security AI Module is ARUHAN’s protection and enforcement layer, responsible for:

system integrity monitoring

access‑control enforcement

anomaly escalation

behavioral containment

lockdown management

cross‑module threat detection

It operates above the Stability & Recovery Engine and alongside the Logging & Telemetry Engine.

This module does not generate user‑facing output.
It protects ARUHAN from internal instability, unauthorized modification, and unsafe operational states.

2. Security Architecture
The Security AI contains five core subsystems:

integrity monitor — detects structural inconsistencies

access controller — enforces module‑level permissions

threat evaluator — identifies harmful patterns

containment engine — isolates unstable modules

lockdown controller — escalates to system lockdown

Each subsystem is deterministic and rule‑driven.

3. Integrity Monitor
The integrity monitor validates:

module signatures

pipeline order

meta‑state ranges

drift/coherence alignment

memory/knowledge consistency

task queue sanity

It compares expected values against real‑time telemetry.

If deviations exceed thresholds, the monitor triggers:

warnings

anomaly flags

containment sequences

4. Access Controller
The access controller enforces strict permissions:

modules may only read/write their designated fields

orchestrator may not modify emotional or memory internals

task engine may not alter knowledge graph structure

stability engine may not modify emotional state directly

external modules may not bypass orchestrator

Access violations trigger:

immediate containment

security log entries

potential lockdown

5. Threat Evaluator
The threat evaluator detects:

runaway emotional drift

recursive memory loops

unstable knowledge graph growth

task queue overload

orchestrator desynchronization

repeated anomaly patterns

Threat levels:

low — logged only

medium — containment triggered

high — lockdown triggered

Threat evaluation is based on:

drift velocity

coherence collapse rate

load acceleration

anomaly frequency

6. Containment Engine
Containment isolates unstable modules without shutting down the system.

Containment actions include:

freezing module output

reducing module influence

forcing safe defaults

blocking write operations

redirecting signals to stability engine

Containment is temporary and reversible.

Containment ends when:

drift stabilizes

coherence recovers

load normalizes

anomalies stop

7. Lockdown Controller
Lockdown is the highest level of system protection.

Triggered when:

drift > 0.90

coherence < 0.20

repeated high‑severity anomalies

orchestrator desync

memory/knowledge corruption detected

Lockdown actions:

freeze all modules

disable task execution

restrict dialogue output

force stability engine into hard‑recovery mode

log all activity at maximum detail

Lockdown persists until:

drift < 0.60

coherence > 0.40

no anomalies for 10 cycles

8. Rule Pipeline (Execution Order)
The Security AI executes in this exact order:

read telemetry

integrity check

access‑control validation

threat evaluation

containment (if needed)

lockdown (if needed)

log security event

return security snapshot

Changing the order will break security logic.

9. Logging System
Security logs contain:

integrity failures

access violations

threat levels

containment events

lockdown events

recovery confirmations

Logs are stored separately from system logs for audit purposes.

10. Debugging Entry Points
Safe to inspect:

threat level

containment status

lockdown status

integrity flags

access‑control violations

Safe to modify:

thresholds

containment intensity

threat scoring weights

Do NOT modify:

lockdown conditions

access‑control rules

integrity signature formats

pipeline order

11. Safe Modification Zones
Engineers may safely:

add new threat types

adjust detection thresholds

expand containment actions

add new integrity checks

Engineers must NOT:

weaken access‑control rules

disable lockdown

bypass containment

remove integrity checks

12. Expansion Guidelines
When expanding security capabilities:

maintain deterministic behavior

avoid circular dependencies

ensure compatibility with the System Orchestrator

test under extreme drift and load

preserve containment isolation

Future versions will integrate:

external watchdogs

cryptographic module signatures

predictive threat modeling

13. Internal Philosophy
The Security AI is built on:

protection

containment

determinism

resilience

strict enforcement

It is not designed to “help” ARUHAN — it is designed to protect ARUHAN from itself and from external interference.

14. Notes for Trusted Engineers
Security AI is the most sensitive module in the system.

All modifications must be tested under simulated failure conditions.

Never disable security in production mode.

Lockdown behavior will expand in Phase 3.
